course: dictionary
lesson: 898
title: Bit: one word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word bit has several meanings, so you need context to know which one the writer means. Read the words around it. «She breaks off a bit of the edge with each hand» tells you a bit is a small piece of something.
explain: A bit can also mean a little or slightly. In «a bit tired», the word bit softens the feeling: the person is tired, but not very tired.
explain: Look for bite. Bit is also the past tense of bite. In «the dog bit him», the word is about something that happened in the past, not a piece or an amount. Ask yourself: what word fits here? Then swap your meaning in and check it still makes sense.
fact: A bit can be a small piece or a small amount: «a bit of cake».
fact: «A bit» means a little or slightly, as in «a bit tired».
fact: «Wait a bit» means wait a short time.
fact: Bit is the past tense of bite: «the dog bit him».
fact: In computers, a bit is the smallest unit of information, a 0 or a 1. Eight bits make a byte.
exercise: «Goats are curious browsers that nibble a bit of everything, but they do not really eat tin cans.» In this sentence, bit means | The past tense of bite | A horse's metal bar | A short time | *A small amount
exercise: «The dog bit him.» In this sentence, bit is | A word meaning a little | A small piece | *The past tense of bite | A metal bar in a horse's mouth
exercise: In computing, what is the smallest unit of information? | A word | *A bit | A byte | A sentence
test: «I'm a bit tired.» What does a bit mean here? | A small piece of tired | *A little, slightly | A long time | The past tense of bite
test: «Water with impurities has tiny bits of other things mixed in it.» What are the bits? | Bites | *Small pieces | Dogs | Computer data
exam: «You smell cookies baking from the other room because tiny invisible bits of the cookies have floated through the air.» What are the bits? | Teeth marks | Bites from a dog | *Tiny pieces | Computer data
